alse, "excerpt": "It is, as Galen saith, moderately hot and dry, and somewhat of thin parts.", "gloss": "Gerard reports Galen: cleavers is moderately hot and dry.", "locator": "Book II, Chap. 461 'Of Goose-Grass, or Cleavers', The Temperature", "not_counted_why": "A second-hand report: the text credits another writer.", "note": "", "short": "Gerard (1633)", "source": "John Gerard, The Herball or Generall Historie of Plantes, enlarged by Thomas Johnson (1633 text; read in the Ex-Classics transcription and checked against the 1636 printing scanned at archive.org)", "tier": "second-hand-report", "tradition": "Galenic (European)", "url": "https://www.exclassics.com/herbal/herball.txt", "year": "1633"}, {"agreement": "Counted: differs from the app temperature (source warm, app cool); differs from the app moisture (source dry, app moist).", "assigns_moisture": "Dry", "assigns_temperature": "Warm", "corpus": "galenic-european", "counted": true, "counted_moisture": true, "counted_temperature": true, "excerpt": "Aparine. Goose-grass, or Clevers: They are meanly hot and dry, cleansing, help the bitings of venomous beasts", "gloss": "Cleavers is moderately hot and dry.", "locator": "Catalogue of simples, Herbs, 'Aparine' (Gutenberg #49513, offset ~844618)", "not_counted_why": "", "note": "", "short": "Culpeper (1653)", "source": "Nicholas Culpeper, The Complete Herbal (text of 1653; London: Thomas Kelly, 1850 edition as digitised by Project Gutenberg #49513)", "tier": "first-hand", "tradition": "Galenic (European)", "url": "https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/49513/pg49513.txt", "year": "1653"}, {"agreement": "Shown for context. A second-hand report: the text credits another writer.", "assigns_moisture": "Dry", "assigns_temperature": "Warm", "corpus": "galenic-european", "counted": false, "counted_moisture": false, "counted_temperature": false, "excerpt": "The Qualities. It is hot and dry in the beginning of the firſt Degree, according to Galen's Judgment", "gloss": "Salmon g