e, "counted_moisture": true, "counted_temperature": true, "excerpt": "Golden-Rod is hot and dry in the second degree: it cleanseth with a certain astriction or binding quality.", "gloss": "Goldenrod is hot and dry in the second degree.", "locator": "Book II, Chap. 103 'Of Golden-Rod', The Temperature", "not_counted_why": "", "note": "", "short": "Gerard (1633)", "source": "John Gerard, The Herball or Generall Historie of Plantes, enlarged by Thomas Johnson (1633 text; read in the Ex-Classics transcription and checked against the 1636 printing scanned at archive.org)", "tier": "first-hand", "tradition": "Galenic (European)", "url": "https://www.exclassics.com/herbal/herball.txt", "year": "1633"}, {"agreement": "Counted: differs from the app temperature (source warm, app cool); agrees with the app moisture (dry).", "assigns_moisture": "Dry", "assigns_temperature": "Warm", "corpus": "galenic-european", "counted": true, "counted_moisture": true, "counted_temperature": true, "excerpt": "Consolida rubra. Golden Rod: hot and dry in the second degree, cleanses the reins, provokes urine, brings away the gravel", "gloss": "Goldenrod is hot and dry in the second degree.", "locator": "Catalogue of simples, Herbs, 'Consolida rubra' (Gutenberg #49513, offset ~860479)", "not_counted_why": "", "note": "", "short": "Culpeper (1653)", "source": "Nicholas Culpeper, The Complete Herbal (text of 1653; London: Thomas Kelly, 1850 edition as digitised by Project Gutenberg #49513)", "tier": "first-hand", "tradition": "Galenic (European)", "url": "https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/49513/pg49513.txt", "year": "1653"}, {"agreement": "Counted: differs from the app temperature (source warm, app cool); agrees with the app moisture (dry).", "assigns_moisture": "Dry", "assigns_temperature": "Warm", "corpus": "galenic-european", "counted": true, "counted_moisture": true, "counted_temperature": true, "excerpt": "VIII. The Qualities. Golden-Rod is hot and dry in the ſecond Degree; Aperitive, Abſter